PR events without the ledger. `observe_pr` no longer calls `once()`. The event log already decides whether an event was written: the existing check `any(r.get('type') == kind and r.get('pr') == pr['number'] for r in self.rows())` (:71) stays and is the only dedup. Changes:
- When that check finds the row, `observe_pr` also deletes any retry token for this PR and kind (below) and returns.
- Otherwise, if the retry token is not due (`retry_due`, bin/lib/fm_autopilot_branches.py:28-32), it returns without emitting.
- Otherwise it calls `self.emit(...)` exactly as today (same kind, task, bilingual text, pr, `actor='github'`). On success it deletes the retry token and, when a poll is in progress (`getattr(self, '_poll_rows', None) is not None`, the same defensive read `rows()` uses at bin/lib/fm_autopilot_loop.py:25, because `_poll_rows` exists only inside `poll()`, bin/lib/fm_autopilot.py:425 and :471, and `observe_pr` is also called outside a poll), appends `dict(type=kind, pr=pr['number'], task=task)` to `self._poll_rows`. The poll caches its rows, and `closed_pull` can reach the same PR twice in one poll (the recent-closures loop at :436 and the per-PR loop at :444-445), so without this append the second call would emit a duplicate within the poll; `once()` used to prevent that.
- On an exception from the tuple `ERRORS` (bin/lib/fm_autopilot_branches.py:10) it calls `branch_failure` with kind `'event-' + kind`, the PR number and the PR head SHA, task and `str(error)`. The token is therefore `event-<kind>:<number>:<head>`, retries come at poll offsets 0, 1 and 3, and the third failure queues one wake '<task> #<number> event-<kind> failed after 3 attempts: <error>'. The first failure's `due_seq` is the next poll, so a second call in the same poll does not retry.
- `pr_task()` (:57-65) stays outside this handling, as today.
Terminal marking stays exactly as today; a pending event write is tracked separately. `closed_pull` (bin/lib/fm_autopilot.py:484-495) still sets `data['pulls'][number]['terminal'] = True` at :492 on its first call, so readers of `terminal` (bin/lib/fm_merge_authorization.py:47-50 and :66) see a merged PR as finished at once, as today; that file is not edited. A new field on the same entry, `event_pending` (the event kind, 'merged' or 'closed'), marks a terminal PR whose event row is still being retried:
- `prune_branches` (bin/lib/fm_autopilot_branches.py:13-22) gains an optional keyword `keep=None`, one token it leaves in place; every other caller passes nothing and behaves exactly as today.
- First `closed_pull` for a PR (entry not yet `terminal`): call `observe_pr` as today. If the token of the PR's current terminal kind (`event-merged:<n>:<head>` or `event-closed:<n>:<head>`, kind as derived at bin/lib/fm_autopilot_loop.py:69) now exists with a count below 3, set `event_pending = kind` and call `prune_branches(number, keep=<that token>)`; otherwise call `prune_branches(number)` as today. A stale `event-pr_opened` token from before the PR closed is pruned either way. Then set `terminal` as today.
- Later `closed_pull` on a `terminal` entry: if `event_pending` is not set, it returns at once without calling `observe_pr`, queueing or pruning. A terminal PR therefore never starts a new retry series: after the third failure (or a success) nothing more is emitted, even while the PR is still in the 50-item recent-closures list, whose entries pass `observed_closure` because they are in `data['pulls']` (:474). If `event_pending` is set, it calls `observe_pr` (which retries only when due), and when the token is gone (success) or has reached count 3 (the wake is queued), it deletes `event_pending` and calls `prune_branches(number)`, so no retry record outlives the series.
- The 'tracked but not open' loop (:438-440) also visits terminal entries that have `event_pending`: its condition becomes `number not in open_numbers and (not previous.get('terminal') or previous.get('event_pending'))`. It fetches `pulls/<n>` and calls `closed_pull`, so the retries at offsets 1 and 3 run even after the PR has left the recent list. A PR first seen already closed through the recent list gets its `data['pulls']` entry from today's `setdefault` at :492, so it is visited too.
- `event()` (:527-530), which marks a PR terminal from a local `merged` row, needs no change: that row is the event, so nothing is pending.
With a head (an open PR), an `event-pr_opened` token for an older head is pruned by `pull()` as today. Tests (stubs as below):
